// R11: the arm bitmap. Filled ONCE at a NAMED init point, never lazily inside live_calls()
// -- a live_calls() that consults an unfilled bitmap binds `original` for rows meant to be
// armed, and the run then reports a clean determinism trajectory that proves nothing. That is
// the G104 shape; arming sets the loaded flag and armed() complains loudly rather than
// defaulting quietly.
//
// FORK F2E COLLAPSED THE GATE AND KEPT THE BITMAP. The `[rebind]` section and its ship-default
// constant are gone -- which rows bind ours is `[config] mode` and nothing else -- so the fill
// takes a bool the caller derived from the selector. The bitmap stays because arming is NOT
// uniform for a non-configuration reason: a row whose ORIGINAL entry some instrument claimed
// (the wall-clock pin, a harness trajectory detour) must NOT be rebound, since a rebound caller
// calls our body directly and never reaches the hook. harness.cpp clears those rows through
// set_armed() after arming; see state/rebind_arming.cpp.
void        arm_from_config(bool ours);
// The offline oracle's arm: nothing, by design. net_selftest drives module bodies through
// recording stubs, and an armed row would rebind a callee underneath them. Distinct from
// arm_from_config(false) so a reader does not go looking for the ini that selected it.
void        arm_none();
// Force one row on/off AFTER arming, by name; false = no such row. For an owner that
// only becomes known later -- the wall-clock pin claims time_GetCurrentTime's ENTRY, so a
// direct rebind of that row would bypass the pin (the promotion arm already yields the same
// way, via MH_Harness_WantsWallclockPin).
bool        set_armed(const char *name, bool on);
bool        armed(int row);
int         armed_count();
const char *row_name(int row);
// The affirmative arming report: count + names, printed even when the count is zero (R11).
void        report_arming(void (*sink)(const char *));

// R6: the deferred set (0 rows) -- bodies LIFT-TACT/LIFT-RESID re-cut. Named traps:
// report the callee by name, then fail fast. LIB-REBIND-UI retires them.
int         trap_count();
const char *trap_name(int i);

